next question today is from Lopez Lopez  says won't businesses just move to other  countries if we increase taxes on them  basically the answer is no not in any  statistically significant way this is a  very common canard that's floated by the  anti-tax right in the United States and  maybe also in Europe all the way I  follow that less closely the idea here  is if you are a corporation and the  government increases your taxes you  would be incentivized to move your  operation to some other country that has  a lower tax rate there's a couple things  that we have to discuss there but let's  start with the sort of most basic I mean  American companies mostly are American  yes there are multinationals which  already are doing business all over the  world and they can more easily move  their headquarters if not all physical  presence but what you have to remember  is a lot of those companies have already  established all sorts of tax shelters  and havens so that's not going to  dramatically change if they've already  determined well we do better by having  an Irish headquarters but we still have  the American presence because we have  the United States such a major market  that is one reason why they are not  going to say we're going to relinquish  our position in the United States  altogether the other thing you have to  remember is even though you might be  able to avoid some American business  taxes if you move your operation to  another country then you risk having  added tariffs if you still want to do  business with American consumers and  corporations and that in some cases may  end up sort of zeroing out the tax  benefit but most importantly all but the  biggest businesses are simply not going  to move to other countries if you  consider all small businesses and even  most medium-sized businesses that  operate primarily in the United States  if you have a huge roofing company or a  construction company not like a local  construction company but the big real  estate companies and you basically work  in the US why are you gonna move to  another country doesn't make any sense  it's logistically significant oh it's  it's logistically significant  undertaking and you're an American  company with American custom  an all-american speaking American  English speaking employees so it's just  not practical to think that that's what  people are going to do now  we actually have some data where we can  analyze this because the same claim has  been made for a really long time about  rich people it's easier for a rich  person to move to another country or to  a lower tax state than it is for a huge  company to move from one country to  another and it turns out that  millionaires don't move very much  millionaires actually move way less than  poor people about half the rate only 2.4  percent of millionaires move in any  given year versus 4.5% of poorer  Americans and interestingly when  millionaires do decide to move they  slightly favor lower tax states slightly  many millionaires are moving to higher  tax states many millionaires move to the  states with the same level of tax and  some millionaires move to lower tax  states and for 85 percent of them the  tax rate in the state they are moving to  is not an important consideration if you  go from looking at millionaires to  looking at billionaires 84% of  billionaires live in their country of  birth period and there's no solid  evidence that tax rates are influencing  them in terms of where it is that they  want to live but here's the really  important part eighty four percent of  new billionaires live in their birth  country 16 percent of billionaires don't  live in their birth country but almost  all of them moved to that country before  they were billionaires meaning that if  the tax rate was a consideration it was  a consideration in some vague distant  way if they ever are billionaires they'd  be better off in one country versus  another rather than I'm moving now  because I want to save money on taxes so  of course when you get to the margins  you have to consider if a government  puts ridiculously high taxes on  corporations it is going to serve as  more of an incentive to move country for  that reason what we're talking about in  the United States about eliminate  loopholes and write-offs and some of  these other mechanisms that exist for  corporations to end up paying almost no  taxes or sometimes no taxes or even  getting tax credits even though they  made profit for the most part these are  changes well within the sort of  reasonable that are not going to cause  those businesses to relocate I'm sure  you can find individual instances of  anything in a country of 330 million  people but from a macroeconomic level  it's been widely widely debunked
 

